{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n16 \n \n \nof the as-built standard and BRE radiometer arrays are found in section 3.F of the ACME \nEngineering Implementation report.   \n \nUnfortunately, the radiometers in the BRE array typically saturated in the first round of BRE \ntesting, so the standard array was used in its second round.  However, those radiometers similarly \nsaturated in many of round 2 tests.  In this regard, each of ACME\u2019s BRE and standard radiometers \nhave a fixed gain.  \n \nRadiometers in the standard ACME array regrettably saturated in most of Flame Design\u2019s first \nround of testing so it was replaced with the CFI-G array in the second round. \n \nFor more information about the standard radiometer array used for most ACME testing, see the \nRadiometer Characterization Report (ACME-RPT-011).  Meanwhile, you can learn about the array \nused in the first round of BRE testing from the BRE Radiometer Characterization Report (ACME-\nRPT-012).    \n \nAn additional wide-view radiometer, that is fixed and not crew exchangeable, is positioned \ndownstream of the burner and flame at 30o from the chamber axis.  This detector is primarily \nintended to support the spherical flame experiments and will have a quicker response than the \nmore sensitive detectors that will used in the linear arrays.   Like the radiometers on the two linear \narrays, it will have a spectral range of nominally 0.2-11 micron and its field of view will be \nestablished by an appropriately selected aperture. \n \nThe FOV of the off-set radiometer is also shown in section 3.F of the ACME Engineering \nImplementation report.  Learn more about this sensor from the Radiometer Characterization \nReport (ACME-RPT-011).   \n \nTo detect the quenching of dim flames, the chamber insert will be equipped with a set of three \nPMTs.  Each PMT will have a wide-angle view, a sight line that is orthogonal to the chamber axis, \nand will be centered (along the chamber length) relative to the chamber windows so that it is \naligned with most of the other instrumentation.  The PMTs\u2019 spectral range will be 230-700 nm, \ni.e., visible and near ultraviolet, but one will have a 310-nm bandpass filter and another will have \na 430-nm bandpass filter (where both are with a 10-nm FWHM).  The pair of detectors will enable \nmeasurement of OH* and CH* emission, respectively.  The third PMT will be equipped with a \nneutral density filter to prevent saturation.  Like many other components, the PMT array can be", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 16}}